  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":37,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A shoe shop records the sizes sold in a day:\n\n3, 4, 4, 5, 5, 5, 5, 6, 6, 7, 8, 8, 9\n\n(a) State the mode and the median.\n(b) Calculate the mean.\n(c) Explain which measure should guide the shop''s ordering decision, and why.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A shoe shop records the sizes sold in a day:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3, 4, 4, 5, 5, 5, 5, 6, 6, 7, 8, 8, 9"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the mode and the median.\n(b) Calculate the mean.\n(c) Explain which measure should guide the shop''s ordering decision, and why."}]}]},"e":"Mode 5, median 5, mean 75/13 ≈ 5.77. The mean is pulled up by the sizes 7, 8, 8 and 9, so for stocking decisions the mode is the right measure: it identifies the size with the highest demand.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"mean, mode and median","st":"mean, mode and median","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":37,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Choose the measure that fits the decision: the mode for the most common category, the median when there are outliers.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the mode.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The mode is the value that appears most often.","Count how many times each size occurs."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":5,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the median.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["With thirteen values the median is the 7th in order.","Count in from the lowest size."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":5,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the mean, to 2 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Add all thirteen sizes.","Divide by 13."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":5.77,"tolerance":0.02},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State which measure should guide ordering and explain why.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["The shop needs the size most often requested.","Does the mean overstate demand for any size?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["the mode of 5, because it is the size most often sold and the shop should stock what customers actually ask for; the mean of 5.77 would over-order larger sizes"],"keywords":[["mode"],["size"],["often"],["sold"],["shop"],["stock"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the total 75, 1 for 5.77. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the choice, 1 for citing demand, 1 for explaining why the mean misleads.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the total 75, 1 for 5.77. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the choice, 1 for citing demand, 1 for explaining why the mean misleads.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Draw the bar chart in the workspace to see the demand by size, then answer (d) with the figures.","statistics_chart":"bar_chart"},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the total 75, 1 for 5.77. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the choice, 1 for citing demand, 1 for explaining why the mean misleads."},{"n":38,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A histogram of waiting times is described as: ''The bar for 0 ≤ t < 10 has height 3 and the bar for 10 ≤ t < 30 has height 1.'' (a) Calculate the frequency of each class.\n(b) Explain why a reader might wrongly think there are three times as many people in the first class.\n(c) Calculate the area of each bar and state what the areas show.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A histogram of waiting times is described as: ''The bar for 0 ≤ t < 10 has height 3 and the bar for 10 ≤ t < 30 has height 1.'' (a) Calculate the frequency of each class.\n(b) Explain why a reader might wrongly think there are three times as many people in the first class.\n(c) Calculate the area of each bar and state what the areas show."}]}]},"e":"Heights 3 and 1 over widths 10 and 20 give frequencies 30 and 20, a ratio of 3 : 2, not 3 : 1. The areas of the bars are the frequencies.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"histograms","st":"histograms","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":38,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"In a histogram, compare areas, not heights. Frequency = density × class width.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the frequency of 0 ≤ t < 10.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Frequency is the area of the bar: density multiplied by class width.","The class is 10 wide and 3 high."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":30,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the frequency of 10 ≤ t < 30.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The class 10 ≤ t < 30 is 20 units wide.","Multiply that width by the height 1."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":20,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why reading heights alone is misleading here.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare the two class widths.","What does the height represent in a histogram?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["the bars have different widths, so height is density not frequency; reading the heights suggests three times as many when it is only one and a half times"],"keywords":[["bars"],["different"],["widths"],["height"],["density"],["frequency"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State what the areas of the two bars represent and give their values.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Area = width × height.","Compare the areas with your frequencies."],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["the area of each bar is its frequency, so the areas are 30 and 20"],"keywords":[["area"],["frequency"],["areas"]],"minKeywordGroups":3}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 30. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the width 20, 1 for 20. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the widths, 1 for the 3 : 2 explanation. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''areas = frequencies'', 1 for both values.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 30. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the width 20, 1 for 20. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the widths, 1 for the 3 : 2 explanation. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''areas = frequencies'', 1 for both values.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Enter the grouped table in the workspace; the chart will plot the densities for you to check.","statistics_chart":"histogram"},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 30. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the width 20, 1 for 20. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the widths, 1 for the 3 : 2 explanation. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''areas = frequencies'', 1 for both values."},{"n":39,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A machine fills bags labelled ''500 g''. A sample of five bags weighs 496, 498, 500, 502 and 504 g.\n\n(a) Calculate the mean mass.\n(b) Calculate the deviations from the mean.\n(c) Calculate the population standard deviation.\n(d) A second sample gives a standard deviation of 6.1 g. Explain what this tells the quality manager, and which sample is closer to the label.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A machine fills bags labelled ''500 g''. A sample of five bags weighs 496, 498, 500, 502 and 504 g."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the mean mass.\n(b) Calculate the deviations from the mean.\n(c) Calculate the population standard deviation.\n(d) A second sample gives a standard deviation of 6.1 g. Explain what this tells the quality manager, and which sample is closer to the label."}]}]},"e":"Mean 500 g; Σ(x − x̄)² = 40; variance 8; σ ≈ 2.83 g. The second sample''s σ of 6.1 g shows far more variability in filling, so it is less precise, although its mean could still be 500 g.","m":9,"df":"hard","tp":"standard deviation","st":"standard deviation","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":39,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"σ measures precision (spread), while the mean measures accuracy (level). Report both when judging a process.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the mean mass, in grams.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Add the five masses and divide by 5.","The total is 2500 g."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":500,"tolerance":0,"unit":"g"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate Σ(x − x̄)² for the sample.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Subtract 500 from each mass, then square.","16 + 4 + 0 + 4 + 16."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":40,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the population standard deviation, to 3 significant figures.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Variance = 40 ÷ 5.","Take the square root of 8."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":2.83,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"g"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain the meaning of σ = 6.1 g for the second sample.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["What does a larger standard deviation measure?","Does the mean have to change as well?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["the second sample''s masses vary more about their mean, so the process is less precise; a standard deviation of 6.1 g is much larger than 2.83 g so that filling is less consistent","it shows greater variability in filling, so the second process is less precise even if its mean is on target"],"keywords":[[["standard deviation","spread","variation","variability","consisten"],["smaller","larger","closer","more","less","compare","mean"]],[["sample","small","few","20 spins","20 seeds","200 spins","variation","chance"],["too small","random","not enough","larger","more trials","expected","difference"]]],"minKeywordGroups":1}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 500 g. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the deviations, 1 for 40. (c) 3 marks: 1 for dividing by 5, 1 for the variance 8, 1 for 2.83. (d) 3 marks: 1 for ''more variable'', 1 for ''less precise'', 1 for separating accuracy from precision.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 500 g. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the deviations, 1 for 40. (c) 3 marks: 1 for dividing by 5, 1 for the variance 8, 1 for 2.83. (d) 3 marks: 1 for ''more variable'', 1 for ''less precise'', 1 for separating accuracy from precision.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Enter the five masses in the workspace as a dot plot to see the symmetry before computing.","statistics_chart":"dot_plot"},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 500 g. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the deviations, 1 for 40. (c) 3 marks: 1 for dividing by 5, 1 for the variance 8, 1 for 2.83. (d) 3 marks: 1 for ''more variable'', 1 for ''less precise'', 1 for separating accuracy from precision."},{"n":40,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A cinema surveys 80 customers about travel time (minutes) to the cinema:\n\n0 ≤ t < 10: 20\n10 ≤ t < 20: 28\n20 ≤ t < 40: 24\n40 ≤ t < 60: 8\n\n(a) Calculate the estimated mean travel time.\n(b) Explain one limitation of the estimated mean compared with the true mean.\n(c) The cinema proposes a free bus for customers who travel more than 20 minutes. Using your table, state how many would qualify, and explain one assumption your answer relies on.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A cinema surveys 80 customers about travel time (minutes) to the cinema:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0 ≤ t < 10: 20\n10 ≤ t < 20: 28\n20 ≤ t < 40: 24\n40 ≤ t < 60: 8"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the estimated mean travel time.\n(b) Explain one limitation of the estimated mean compared with the true mean.\n(c) The cinema proposes a free bus for customers who travel more than 20 minutes. Using your table, state how many would qualify, and explain one assumption your answer relies on."}]}]},"e":"Σf x = 1640 over 80 customers gives 20.5 minutes. The midpoint assumption flattens variation inside the wide classes. Customers over 20 minutes are 24 + 8 = 32, assuming the class boundary matches ''more than 20''.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"grouped data","st":"grouped data","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":40,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"An estimated mean from grouped data is always an approximation; state the midpoint assumption when you interpret it.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate Σ(f × midpoint).","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Midpoints are 5, 15, 30 and 50.","Multiply each by its frequency and add."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":1640,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the estimated mean travel time, in minutes.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The estimated mean is Σ(f × midpoint) divided by the total frequency.","Divide your 1640 by the 80 customers."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":20.5,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain one limitation of this estimated mean.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Where does the estimate place every value in a class?","Is that realistic?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["it assumes every value in a class is at the midpoint, so it ignores the real spread within each class and is only an estimate"],"keywords":[["assumes"],["value"],["class"],["midpoint"],["ignores"],["real"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State how many customers travel more than 20 minutes, and one assumption behind it.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Add the two classes above 20 minutes.","Does the table show anyone exactly at 20 minutes?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["32 customers, assuming the 20 ≤ t < 40 class is correctly classified as more than 20 minutes and that each customer answered once"],"keywords":[["customers"],["assuming"],["class"],["correctly"],["classified"],["minutes"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoints, 1 for 1640. (b) 2 marks: 1 for dividing by 80, 1 for 20.5. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoint assumption, 1 for its effect. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 32, 1 for a valid assumption.","marking_criteria":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoints, 1 for 1640. (b) 2 marks: 1 for dividing by 80, 1 for 20.5. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoint assumption, 1 for its effect. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 32, 1 for a valid assumption.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Draw the histogram in the workspace (the 20-minute and 20-wide classes need densities) and use it in (c).","statistics_chart":"histogram"},"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoints, 1 for 1640. (b) 2 marks: 1 for dividing by 80, 1 for 20.5. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoint assumption, 1 for its effect. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 32, 1 for a valid assumption."},{"n":41,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A school records the number of minutes 50 students spend on homework. The cumulative frequency curve shows the median at 42 minutes, Q1 at 28 minutes and Q3 at 58 minutes.\n\n(a) State the interquartile range.\n(b) Explain what the IQR tells the school that the median alone does not.\n(c) A teacher claims ''most students work about 42 minutes''. Evaluate the claim using the quartiles.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A school records the number of minutes 50 students spend on homework. The cumulative frequency curve shows the median at 42 minutes, Q1 at 28 minutes and Q3 at 58 minutes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the interquartile range.\n(b) Explain what the IQR tells the school that the median alone does not.\n(c) A teacher claims ''most students work about 42 minutes''. Evaluate the claim using the quartiles."}]}]},"e":"IQR = 30 minutes. The median locates the middle student; the IQR shows the middle half span 28–58 minutes, so the teacher''s ''about 42 minutes'' is a central value that conceals wide variation.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"cumulative frequency","st":"cumulative frequency","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":41,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Pairs of measures tell the whole story: a measure of centre plus a measure of spread.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the interquartile range, in minutes.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["IQR = Q3 − Q1.","58 − 28."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":30,"tolerance":0,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Explain what the IQR adds to the median.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["What does the IQR measure?","Which half of the students does it describe?"],"strand":"D(ii)","accepted":["the IQR describes the spread of the middle half of the students, whereas the median gives only one central value"],"keywords":[["describes"],["spread"],["middle"],["half"],["students"],["whereas"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Evaluate the teacher''s claim using the quartiles.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Half the students work more than 42 minutes and half less.","How wide is the range of the middle half?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["the median does show a typical student at 42 minutes, but the middle half spread over 28 to 58 minutes, so ''about 42 minutes'' hides a wide variation"],"keywords":[["median"],["show"],["typical"],["student"],["minutes"],["middle"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 30. (b) 2 marks: 1 for ''spread of the middle half'', 1 for the contrast with the median. (c) 3 marks: 1 for accepting the median value, 1 for citing 28–58, 1 for judging the claim''s limitation.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 30. (b) 2 marks: 1 for ''spread of the middle half'', 1 for the contrast with the median. (c) 3 marks: 1 for accepting the median value, 1 for citing 28–58, 1 for judging the claim''s limitation.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Quote the quartiles in (c) — an evaluation without figures is just an opinion."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 30. (b) 2 marks: 1 for ''spread of the middle half'', 1 for the contrast with the median. (c) 3 marks: 1 for accepting the median value, 1 for citing 28–58, 1 for judging the claim''s limitation."}]'::jsonb) as x
